/-!
# The reciprocal sum of the prime factors when exactly one of `3`, `5` divides `N`

For an odd perfect number `N` we prove

* `3 ∣ N`, `5 ∤ N`: `0.60470764 < ∑_{p ∣ N} 1/p` (`sum_inv_gt_60470764_of_three_not_five`);
* `3 ∤ N`, `5 ∣ N`: `0.64764966 < ∑_{p ∣ N} 1/p` (`sum_inv_gt_64764966_of_five_not_three`).

These replace the constants `0.604706` and `0.647648` of `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFour`.

Comparison with the literature. The rows are the four classes of `gcd(N, 15)`; each entry is a
lower bound and an upper bound for `∑_{p ∣ N} 1/p`, read from the source.

* `1970`: the bounds of Suryanarayana and Hagis (1970), as reprinted in Table 1 (page 370) of
  G. L. Cohen, "On odd perfect numbers (II), multiperfect numbers and quasiperfect numbers",
  J. Austral. Math. Soc. Ser. A 29 (1980), 369 to 384.
* `1980`: Table 2 (page 370) of the same paper, Cohen's improved bounds.
* `2024`: Proposition 1 (page 3) of J. Zelinsky, "The sum of the reciprocals of the prime
  divisors of an odd perfect or odd primitive non-deficient number", arXiv:2402.14234 (version
  5, 2025), which summarizes the results of Cohen, Hagis and Suryanarayana; its values are those
  of the column `1970`.
* `proved`: `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFour`, `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumUpper`,
  `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFive` and this file.

| gcd(N, 15) | 1970               | 1980               | 2024               | proved               |
|------------|--------------------|--------------------|--------------------|----------------------|
| 15         | 0.596063, 0.673770 | 0.596063, 0.673634 | 0.596063, 0.673770 | 0.5960631, 0.673634  |
| 3          | 0.603831, 0.657304 | 0.604707, 0.657304 | 0.603831, 0.657304 | 0.60470764, 0.657304 |
| 5          | 0.647387, 0.678036 | 0.647649, 0.677637 | 0.647387, 0.678036 | 0.64764966, 0.677637 |
| 1          | 0.667450, 0.693148 | 0.667472, 0.693148 | 0.667450, 0.693148 | 0.66747288, log 2    |

In the classes `3` and `5` of this file the constants `0.60470764` and `0.64764966` are ahead of
the lower bounds `0.603831` and `0.647387` of Proposition 1 of Zelinsky's preprint, and ahead of
Cohen's printed values `0.604707` and `0.647649`. Cohen's bounds come from his Theorem 1 (iv)
and (i) (page 372), `S₂(3, 7, 11, 29, 331; 337)`, about `0.6047074912`, and
`S₂(5, ..., 19, 41, 907; 911)`, about `0.6476496541` (evaluated here in floating point from his
formula); the constants here are ahead of these values in the seventh and in the ninth decimal
place. On page 379 Cohen shows that without new restrictions on the prime factors or their
exponents his bounds can be improved at best up to the limits `S₂(...; ∞)`, about
`0.6047076458` and `0.6476496610`. The bounds here use no new restriction and stay below these
limits. Computations outside Lean put the infimum of the relaxed problem (a set of odd primes
containing `3` and not `5`, or `5` and not `3`, with `log 2 < ∑ log(p/(p-1))`; minimize
`∑ 1/p`) in the interval from `0.6047076401` to `0.6047076459` in the first class and from
`0.6476496606` to `0.6476496611` in the second (an exact mirror of the check below with cutoff
`60000`, and explicit finite sets of primes). So at most about `6 * 10^(-9)` and `10^(-9)` are
left above the constants of this file for this method.

The method is that of `OddPerfect.ReciprocalSumFive`. Only `log 2 < ∑_{p ∣ N} log(p/(p-1))`
(`log_two_lt_sum_log`) is used; in a floating point search with cutoff `1000` the condition on
the abundancy did not change the relaxed infimum in these classes. The check `sixCheck` walks through the odd primes `p < 10000` in increasing order and
decides for each whether it divides `N` (the prime of `req` must, the prime of `exc` must not).
A branch stops as soon as `t < r` or, with `m` the next entry of the list (or `m = 10000` at the
end), `t ≤ r + (2m - 4)/(2m - 3) * (10^15 log 2 - u)`, where `r` and `u` are the scaled sums of
`1/p` (rounded down) and of the upper bounds `u(p)/10^15 ≥ log(p/(p-1))` (from `sixTable`) over
the decided prime factors. The cover of the primes is checked by `sixGap`. The table was
computed outside Lean (generator `scripts/ReciprocalSum/gen_recip6.py`); the kernel checks again
every table entry, every gap and every branch.
-/

open Finset

namespace OddPerfect

